10357 0 obj
  << /ActualText (a brilliant programmer\054 he was a teenager deeply immersed in Blizzard\031s MMORPG3\051\054 World of Warcraft \050WoW\051\056 He meticulously studied his skill tree and developed his character with care\056 Then\054 one day\054 Blizzard nerfed the skill he valued most\072 Siphon Life\056 Vitalik later recalled the shock of that moment\072\n\034I cried myself to sleep that night\056 And I realized with bone\055deep clarity how terrifying the power wielded by centralized services could be\056\035IV\nHe saw through the core problem\056 In digital worlds\054 no matter how much value users build\054 a structural anxiety remains\072 if the centralized authority controlling that world changes the rules\054 that value can collapse at any moment\056 This is a form of digital totalitarianism\056\n\nThis anxiety and fear are not problems unique to games\056 When viewed at a macro level\054 they reveal a fundamental contradiction embedded in the development of the internet itself\024one that defines the era of Web 2\0560 in which we live\056 If the early internet\054 Web 1\0560\054 was an era of reading\024one in which users consumed information unilaterally\024Web 2\0560 marked a revolution of writing\054 enabling users to participate directly and produce content\056 We write blog posts\054 upload videos to YouTube\054 and generate vast amounts of data\056 Platforms grant us the freedom to participate\054 thereby enriching the internet\056 Yet a fatal trap lies within this model\056 While users gained the right to write\024to create\024they did not gain ownership of what they produced\056 The rights to this data\024memories posted on Instagram\054 creative works uploaded to YouTube\054 and even the time invested in World of Warcraft\024remain firmly in the hands of platform companies\056)
     /K [ 2 ]
     /P 10358 0 R
     /Pg 2632 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

