10401 0 obj
  << /ActualText (In 2020\054 the world came to a standstill\056 The COVID\05519 pandemic issued a global command to \034stay home\054\035 and borders and storefronts shut down simultaneously\056 While this order translated into Netflix and remote work for citizens of developed nations\054 for daily wage workers in developing countries such as the Philippines\054 it meant something far more severe\072 starvation\056 Manila\031s streets emptied\054 street vendors lost their livelihoods\054 and tricycle drivers saw their incomes collapse\056\n\nAmid the ruins of a world where physical means of earning had vanished\054 an unexpected digital lifeboat emerged\072 Axie Infinity\054 a blockchain\055based game developed by the Vietnamese startup Sky Mavis\056 At first glance\054 the game appeared deceptively simple\024players collected cute monsters\054 known as \034Axies\054\035 and pitted them against one another in battles\056 Yet for many Filipinos\054 Axie Infinity was far more than entertainment\056 It became a primary means of survival\054 a digital production tool substituting for a collapsed real\055world economy\056\nThe spark that ignited this movement was the widely publicized success story of a 22\055year\055old named John Aaron Ramos\056 When reports spread across social media and news outlets that he had used his in\055game earnings to buy his family a house\054 the entire Philippines began to buzz\056V The story echoed an earlier Korean legend of a Lineage player who sold an Executioner Sword to purchase a car\056 Cabanatuan City\054 where the Ramos family\031s miracle took place\054 was soon dubbed the \034capital of Axie\056\035VI The city effectively transformed into a vast digital mining site\056 Fathers who had lost their jobs during lockdowns\054 students with nowhere to go after schools closed\054 and even elderly residents unfamiliar with smartphones\024all crowded around small screens\054 hunting monsters\056 What drove them was not the joy of play or the thrill of victory\054 but the yellow potion bottle awarded after each battle\072 the cashable token SLP \050Smooth Love Potion\051\056\n\nAt the peak of Axie Infinity\031s popularity\054 monthly earnings from the game reached between five hundred and seven hundred dollars\056VII Compared to the wages earned by many daily laborers in the Philippines\054 this appeared to be a digital El Dorado\024a promise of higher income than spending long hours laboring under the scorching sun\054 achieved instead by tapping on a smartphone in front of an air conditioner or electric fan\056\n\nThis boom reshaped the structure of labor itself\056 For those lacking the initial capital required to participate\054 a so\055called \034scholarship\035 model emerged\056 \034Managers\035 lent Axie teams4\051 to players and claimed a share of the rewards generated through gameplay\056VIII Despite its name\054 the arrangement closely resembled a form of sharecropping\054 in which a landlord provides tools to tenant )
     /K [ 7 ]
     /P 10402 0 R
     /Pg 3120 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

