10430 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The core lesson revealed by the Axie Infinity craze in the Philippines is clear\056 The massive influx of players was not an expansion of play\054 but a migration for survival\056 Philippine players interviewed by TIME magazine said they started playing because they were told they could earn more money\056 Over time\054 however\054 many described the game as \034boring and stressful\056\035IX How creative the deck building5\051 was or how flashy the skill effects6\051 looked became irrelevant\056 The only questions that mattered were\072 \034How much SLP did I earn today\077\035 and \034How much is that worth in pesos\077\035\n\nWhen the standard of judgment changes\054 language changes as well\056 At the time\054 online communities and YouTube were flooded with content focused on profit\055maximization strategies\056 Players began to speak like miners\054 not gamers\056 Just as Bitcoin miners calculate electricity costs and profitability\054 Axie players multiplied their working hours by the SLP market price to calculate their daily wages\056 They \034clocked in\035 only as long as this income was better than other available jobs\056\n\nThis leads us to a key proposition\056 Play\054 Move\054 Study\024whatever X represents\054 the moment crypto rewards are attached to that action\054 X is no longer perceived as play but as mining\056 The essence of Something\055to\055Earn \050X2E\051 lies not in X\054 but in E\024the act of earning\056)
     /K [ 12 ]
     /P 10431 0 R
     /Pg 3676 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

