10524 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Today\054 debates surrounding AI are deeply polarized\056 Yuval Noah Harari\054 the author of Sapiens\054 warns that humanity may\054 \034for the first time in history\054 become a \030useless class\056\031\035XII On the other hand\054 tech optimists such as OpenAI CEO Sam Altman prophesy that \034humanity\054 freed from labor\054 will enter an age of prosperity\056\035XIII While these visions diverge between dystopia and utopia\054 both camps are in ironic agreement on a single premise\072 traditional wage labor is approaching an irreversible end\056\n\nThe real problem lies not in the destination\054 but in the journey\056 Even if an ideal world of full automation and universal basic income eventually arrives\054 getting there will take time\056 Humanity must first cross a dangerous transitional period\024the civilizational chasm\024in which the old order has collapsed\054 but the new safety net has yet to be built\056 Historically\054 such moments of rupture have always been the most brutal for the unprepared majority\056\n\nPast waves of automation primarily replaced human muscle\056 Today\031s AI\054 however\054 delivers precision strikes against the human mind\056 In logistics warehouses\054 robots tirelessly move boxes\073 on highways\054 self\055driving trucks cruise with increasing autonomy\056 These developments were largely expected\056 The true shock arrived when large language models began to encroach on domains once thought secure\072 translation\054 accounting\054 software development\054 and even legal work\056 Technology advances exponentially\054 doubling in capability year after year\054 while laws and institutions remain entangled in vested interests\054 procedures\054 and inertia\056 This widening mismatch\024where jobs disappear faster than society can construct new safety nets\024deepens the chasm and makes the transition increasingly perilous\056)
     /K [ 3 ]
     /P 10525 0 R
     /Pg 5562 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

