10608 0 obj
  << /ActualText (No matter how creative people are\054 if the platform only lets them do simple \034mining\035 work\054 the ecosystem cannot survive\056 Even if many creators join\054 if the system treats them as disposable workers\054 they will eventually leave for better places\056 A sustainable digital world must be built on better\054 more thoughtful system design\056\n\nThe biggest mistake of P2E 1\0560 was that it trusted what could be seen on the screen\056 Players were all hunting monsters and finishing quests\054 and from the outside\054 this looked like success\056 Planners and investors felt safe and told themselves they had \034secured a large number of users\056\035 But in reality\054 most of these users were not there to enjoy the game\056 They were there to earn tokens and sell them\056 Because of this\054 the ecosystem was dominated by people who wanted to cash out\054 not by people who wanted to spend\056 In the end\054 what decides the fate of a platform is not what users are doing\054 but why they logged in\056 This invisible difference turns into two completely opposite economic forces\072 buying pressure \050consumption\051 and selling pressure \050cash\055out\051\056 A system where everyone must earn money in order to participate is\054 in the end\054 just a mine where everyone is getting ready to quit\056\n\nDesigners of X2E 2\0560 must carefully understand why users come to the platform\056 The important question is not\054 \034How can we get more users\077\035 but\054 \034How can we keep a healthy balance of different kinds of users\077\035 An economy can survive only when the desire of settlers to spend and use tokens is stronger than the speed at which miners produce and sell them\056 Instead of using a reward\055focused message like\054 \034Kill this monster and get paid\054\035 the system must switch to a consumption\055focused one\072 \034Use this token and gain fun\054 enjoyment\054 and status\056\035\n\nThe clearest real\055world answer already exists outside blockchain\054 in the largest metaverse of all\072 Roblox\056 The secret of Roblox\031s sustainability is not a clever tokenomics formula\054 but a structural separation of roles that locks different kinds of desire into the system\056 Roblox\031s economy runs on two interlocking groups with different purposes\056 On one side are gamers\054 the consumers\056 They spend real money to buy Robux\056 With Robux\054 they customize avatars\054 buy game passes\054 spend time with friends\054 and purchase experiences\056 Their goal is play\054 not investment\056 On the other side are creators\054 the producers\056 Using Roblox Studio\054 they build games and items and earn Robux from gamers\056XVI When their earnings exceed a certain threshold\054 they can convert Robux into dollars through the Developer Exchange \050DevEx\051\056 Their goal is not play\054 but business\056\n\nThis clear separation of roles determines the platform\031s shape\056 Roblox does not define itself as a single game\054 but as a marketplace of experiences\024a digital department store\056 The platform provides the building \050servers\051\054 electricity )
     /K [ 5 ]
     /P 10609 0 R
     /Pg 6878 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

