10670 0 obj
  << /ActualText (We have examined various cases\054 starting with Lineage in Korea\054 moving through Axie Infinity in the Philippines and RuneScape in Venezuela\054 and extending to the experiments of Roblox and Bonk\056 If we interpret this trajectory merely as the rise and fall of the gaming industry\054 we risk missing the most important signal\056 What we are really seeing is a record of a digital diaspora\024humanity\054 losing its physical foothold\054 wandering in search of new land in the virtual realm\056\n\nPeople commuting into online games are no longer a strange headline\056 As AI and automation continue to replace existing jobs\054 the center of gravity of work will inevitably shift from factories to platforms\054 and from offices to servers\056 In this sense\054 X2E and the metaverse must not remain speculative bubbles that burst and disappear\056 They should be understood as a social buffer zone for humanity\024now facing the risk of mass technological displacement\024and as a new frontier for economic activity\056\n\nAt its core\054 money is proof of labor\056 In the traditional wage system\054 earning money meant going to a specific place at a specific time and receiving proof that \034I contributed this much to society\056\035 But in an era where robots and algorithms replace humans\054 we can no longer prove our value in the old way\056 This does not mean human value disappears\056 Only the stage changes\056 On this new stage\024the virtual world\024some produce resources\054 some consume\054 some create culture\054 and some design worlds\056 The moment we recognize these activities as work\054 they deserve compensation just as much as labor in the physical world\056\n\nThe problem is that this shift does not automatically guarantee a healthy economy\056 The tragedy of P2E 1\0560 came precisely from this point\056 Early projects tried to ground the value of digital labor not in real consumption\054 but in tokens themselves\056 It was a distorted structure\072 demand was weak\054 but supply kept exploding\056 Few people opened their wallets for enjoyment\054 while many logged in only for profit\056 It was like a factory running 24\0577 with no customers\056 When token prices collapsed\054 the digital nation collapsed with them\056\n\nWhat we must now design are the systems and ethics that prevent this failure from repeating\056 Through motivation design\054 we must classify users not by what they do\054 but by why they participate\054 and maintain a healthy balance between nomads and settlers\056 Through ecosystem integration\054 we must anchor economies to proven base currencies instead of isolated tokens\056 Through governance\054 tokenomics must be elevated into transparent monetary policy\054 where user participation and oversight genuinely work\056 Only then can those who \034commute into online games\035 be recognized not as digital proletarians\054 but as digital citizens who receive fair compensation\056\n\nIn the end\054 this is not a story about technology\056 It is a story about humanity\056 As AI brings the era of traditional labor to a close\054 we are being forced to rewrite )
     /K [ 4 ]
     /P 10671 0 R
     /Pg 7996 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

